narrative:
## Making Decisions with Struct Data

Structs hold data, and comparing that data is a common task. Let's write functions that answer questions about our Person struct.

### Comparing by Age

```xiom
type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
}

fn is_older(a: Person, b: Person) -> Bool {
  return a.age > b.age;
}

fn same_age(a: Person, b: Person) -> Bool {
  return a.age == b.age;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let me = Person{ name: "Kai", age: 15 };
  let friend = Person{ name: "Lee", age: 14 };

  io.println(is_older(me, friend));    // true
  io.println(same_age(me, friend));    // false
  return 0;
}
```

### Using Comparison in if

```xiom
fn compare_ages(a: Person, b: Person) {
  if a.age > b.age {
    io.println(a.name + " is older than " + b.name);
  } else {
    if a.age < b.age {
      io.println(b.name + " is older than " + a.name);
    } else {
      io.println(a.name + " and " + b.name + " are the same age");
    }
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let p1 = Person{ name: "Morgan", age: 16 };
  let p2 = Person{ name: "Nico", age: 16 };
  compare_ages(p1, p2);
  return 0;
}
```

### Comparing by Name

Comparisons aren't just for numbers — you can compare any field:

```xiom
fn same_name(a: Person, b: Person) -> Bool {
  return a.name == b.name;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let p1 = Person{ name: "Alex", age: 14 };
  let p2 = Person{ name: "Alex", age: 16 };
  io.println(same_name(p1, p2));  // true — same name, different ages!
  return 0;
}
```

Comparison functions turn raw data into meaningful answers — turning "here are two ages" into "Kai is older than Lee."

